From 0eca13dc77809561e93857df137b77fcfd91c5d7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: StachowiakDawid <dawidos234@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 23 Nov 2026 18:38:09 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] soc: samsung: exynos-pmu: Exynos9820 CPU hotplug and system
 sleep support

- The lookup of a CPU's PMU register block is factored out of the
  Exynos 9820 hotplug/idle callbacks, and a cpu_pmu_powered() callback
  reports whether the PMU sees a core's power domain on;
  exynos_pmu_cpu_powered() exports it for the suspend code.
- exynos_pmu_sys_sleep(): for a firmware system sleep the CPU_PM
  notifier refuses CPU_PM_ENTER from suspend_noirq on, which also
  failed the syscore cpu_pm_suspend(). The platform sleep code sets
  this flag from its own syscore suspend; while it is set
  CPU_PM_ENTER/EXIT pass without touching the C2 PMU setup.
- PSCI v0.1 has no AFFINITY_INFO, so nothing waits for a dying core.
  A core still powering down when the boot CPU entered a SoC sleep
  never booted again. A teardown in the prepare hotplug state polls
  the core power status until it is off, for at most 100 ms.
- Cluster 2 is powered down properly on hotplug, as the vendor kernel
  does: the last CPU of the cluster going offline gets the CPD hint in
  CPU_INFORM (cpd_hint) and affinity level 1 in the PSCI CPU_OFF power
  state (cl2_off_affl), both default on; SYSREG_CPUCL2
  BUS_COMPONENT_DRCG_EN, which a SoC sleep resets, is restored when a
  cluster 2 CPU comes online. A cpu_pmu_pre_on hook runs on the
  control CPU before CPU_ON.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.2 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Signed-off-by: StachowiakDawid <dawidos234@gmail.com>
